---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CountHistogram from '../components/charts/CountHistogram.astro';
import n3Json from '../data/n3.json';
import nullsJson from '../data/nulls.json';
import { wilson } from '../lib/shuffle';

const n3: any = n3Json;
const nulls: any = nullsJson;
const T = ['T1', 'T2', 'T3'] as const;
const NAME: Record<string, string> = { T1: 'the incumbent party wins', T2: 'the Democratic Party wins', T3: 'the Republican Party wins' };
const ACT: Record<string, string> = { T1: 'incumbent party won', T2: 'Democratic Party won', T3: 'Republican Party won' };
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const pct = (x: number, d = 1) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(d)}%`;
const tg = n3.targets;
const trainN = n3.train_years.length, testN = n3.test_years.length;
const tier = (l: string) => (l === 'conjA' ? 'tier 1a' : l === 'conjB' ? 'tier 1b' : 'nothing');
const best = (t: string) => tg[t].baseline.best_constant_correct as number;
const diff = (t: string) => tg[t].top_rule.test_correct - best(t);
const noneBeat = T.every((t) => diff(t) <= 0);
const bins = Array.from({ length: testN + 1 }, (_, i) => ({ key: String(i), label: `${i}/${testN}` }));
const histAll = T.map((t) => ({ id: t, label: t, counts: tg[t].all_rules_hist as Record<string, number>, B: tg[t].n_rules_21yr as number,
  realBin: String(tg[t].top_rule.test_correct), realText: '', baselineBin: String(best(t)) }));
const histNull = T.map((t) => ({ id: t, label: t, counts: tg[t].null.top_correct_hist as Record<string, number>, B: tg[t].null.B as number,
  realBin: String(tg[t].top_rule.test_correct), realText: '' }));
const above = (t: string, strict: boolean) => {
  const base = best(t);
  return Object.entries(tg[t].all_rules_hist as Record<string, number>).filter(([k]) => (strict ? Number(k) > base : Number(k) >= base)).reduce((a, [, v]) => a + v, 0);
};
const nullB = tg.T1.null.B;
const ci = (k: number, n: number) => { const [lo, hi] = wilson(k, n); return `${pct(lo, 1)} to ${pct(hi, 1)}`; };
---
<Base title="The look-ahead test (N3)" description="Fit the first 21 elections, then score the last 11." current="n3">
  <h1>The look-ahead test</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    We hid the last {testN} elections, found rules that fit the first {trainN} perfectly, and then looked.
  </p>

  <h2>The test</h2>
  <p>
    The search was run again on 1900&ndash;1980 only ({trainN} elections), with the same pools, tiers and ranking. A rule counts only if it
    gets all {trainN} of those elections right. The top-ranked rule under the frozen ranking was then scored on 1984&ndash;2024 ({testN} elections), which the
    search had never seen. As yardsticks we use the two dullest possible rules, which ignore the facts entirely: always guess &ldquo;no&rdquo; and always guess &ldquo;yes&rdquo;
    (for T1, the incumbent party always loses or always wins; for T2 and T3, always Republican or always Democratic). The better of the two is the
    <em>best constant guess</em>.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="plain">
      <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Rules that fit 1900&ndash;1980 perfectly</th><th>Top-ranked rule on 1984&ndash;2024</th><th>Always &ldquo;no&rdquo;</th><th>Always &ldquo;yes&rdquo;</th><th>Best constant guess</th><th>Rule minus best guess</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {T.map((t) => {
          const r = tg[t];
          const d = diff(t);
          return (
            <tr>
              <td>{t}: {NAME[t]}</td>
              <td class="num">{fmt(r.n_rules_21yr)}</td>
              <td class="num"><strong>{r.top_rule.test_correct} of {r.top_rule.test_n}</strong></td>
              <td class="num">{r.baseline.always_loss_correct} of {r.baseline.test_n}<span class="sub">{r.baseline.name_no}</span></td>
              <td class="num">{r.baseline.always_win_correct} of {r.baseline.test_n}<span class="sub">{r.baseline.name_yes}</span></td>
              <td class="num"><strong>{best(t)} of {r.baseline.test_n}</strong><span class="sub">{r.baseline.best_constant_name}</span></td>
              <td class="num">{d > 0 ? `+${d}` : d}</td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  {noneBeat && (
    <p>
      <strong>In no case did the top-ranked rule beat the best constant guess.</strong>
      {T.map((t) => diff(t) === 0
        ? <> For {t} it tied ({best(t)} of {testN}, {tg[t].baseline.best_constant_name}).</>
        : <> For {t} it did worse: {tg[t].top_rule.test_correct} against {best(t)} for &ldquo;{tg[t].baseline.best_constant_name}&rdquo;.</>)}
      {' '}It fit the past perfectly and added nothing about the future.
    </p>
  )}

  <h3>The three top-ranked rules, election by election</h3>
  {T.map((t) => {
    const r = tg[t].top_rule;
    return (
      <details class="viz-table">
        <summary>{t}: {NAME[t]}, {r.test_correct} of {r.test_n} (best constant guess, {tg[t].baseline.best_constant_name}: {best(t)})</summary>
        <p class="sub">The rule: all of the following must hold (&ldquo;NOT&rdquo; means the fact must be false).</p>
        <ul>
          {r.glosses.map((g: string, i: number) => (
            <li>{g}{r.defunct[i] && <span class="badge flag" title="Names a club that no longer exists (C2.3)"> defunct club (C2.3)</span>}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="sub">It fits all {trainN} elections of 1900&ndash;1980.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Actual ({ACT[t]})</th><th>Rule says</th><th>Rule right?</th><th>Always &ldquo;no&rdquo; right?</th><th>Always &ldquo;yes&rdquo; right?</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {r.rows.map((row: any) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{row.year}</td><td>{row.actual ? 'yes' : 'no'}</td><td>{row.rule_says ? 'yes' : 'no'}</td>
                  <td><span class={row.correct ? 'yes' : 'no'}>{row.correct ? '✓' : '✗'}</span></td>
                  <td><span class={row.baseline_correct ? 'yes' : 'no'}>{row.baseline_correct ? '✓' : '✗'}</span></td>
                  <td><span class={row.always_yes_correct ? 'yes' : 'no'}>{row.always_yes_correct ? '✓' : '✗'}</span></td>
                </tr>
              ))}
              <tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td></td><td></td><td><strong>{r.test_correct}</strong></td><td><strong>{tg[t].baseline.always_loss_correct}</strong></td><td><strong>{tg[t].baseline.always_win_correct}</strong></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>
    );
  })}

  <h2>Every rule that fit the past</h2>
  <p>
    The top-ranked rule is one of many. {fmt(tg.T1.n_rules_21yr)} rules fit 1900&ndash;1980 perfectly for T1, {fmt(tg.T2.n_rules_21yr)} for T2 and
    {fmt(tg.T3.n_rules_21yr)} for T3. Here is how each one did on 1984&ndash;2024. For T1, {pct(above('T1', true) / tg.T1.n_rules_21yr, 1)} of them beat
    the best constant guess and {pct((tg.T1.n_rules_21yr - above('T1', false)) / tg.T1.n_rules_21yr, 1)} did worse (T2: {pct(above('T2', true) / tg.T2.n_rules_21yr, 1)} beat it,
    {pct((tg.T2.n_rules_21yr - above('T2', false)) / tg.T2.n_rules_21yr, 1)} did worse; T3: {pct(above('T3', true) / tg.T3.n_rules_21yr, 1)} beat it,
    {pct((tg.T3.n_rules_21yr - above('T3', false)) / tg.T3.n_rules_21yr, 1)} did worse).
  </p>
  <CountHistogram series={histAll} bins={bins} title="Test scores of every rule that fit 1900–1980"
    sub="Share of the rules for each target, by how many of the 11 elections 1984–2024 they got right"
    xLabel="elections right out of 11 (1984–2024)" yLabel="share of rules" realLegend="the top-ranked rule" baselineLegend="best constant guess"
    tipPrefix="Score " tipSuffix="" descId="fig-n3-all" />
  <details class="viz-table" open>
    <summary>Data table: test scores of every rule that fit 1900–1980</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Elections right (of {testN})</th>{T.map((t) => <th class="r">{t} rules</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {bins.map((b) => (
            <tr><td>{b.label}</td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r">{fmt(tg[t].all_rules_hist[b.key] ?? 0)} ({pct((tg[t].all_rules_hist[b.key] ?? 0) / tg[t].n_rules_21yr, 1)})</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
          <tr><td><strong>Top-ranked rule</strong></td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r"><strong>{tg[t].top_rule.test_correct}/{testN}</strong></td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Always &ldquo;no&rdquo;</strong></td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r"><strong>{tg[t].baseline.always_loss_correct}/{testN}</strong></td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Always &ldquo;yes&rdquo;</strong></td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r"><strong>{tg[t].baseline.always_win_correct}/{testN}</strong></td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Best constant guess</strong></td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r"><strong>{best(t)}/{testN} ({tg[t].baseline.best_constant_name})</strong></td>)}</tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h3>The 20 top-ranked rules for each target</h3>
  <p class="sub">
    In the frozen ranking&rsquo;s order. Many rules tie on every ranking criterion, so the order among them is alphabetical, not a measure of quality.
    A rule that scores above the best constant guess is marked &#9650;.
  </p>
  {T.map((t) => (
    <details class="viz-table">
      <summary>{t}: {NAME[t]}, the top 20 of {fmt(tg[t].n_rules_21yr)}</summary>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th class="r">Rank</th><th>Facts (all must hold; &ldquo;NOT&rdquo; = must be false)</th><th class="r">Test score</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tg[t].top20.map((r: any) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="r">{r.rank}</td>
                <td>{r.glosses.map((g: string, i: number) => <>{i > 0 && '; '}{g}{r.defunct[i] && <span class="badge flag" title="Names a club that no longer exists (C2.3)"> defunct club</span>}</>)}</td>
                <td class="r">{r.test_correct}/{testN}{r.test_correct > best(t) ? ' ▲' : ''}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
  ))}

  <h2>The same test on shuffled histories</h2>
  <p>
    We ran the identical procedure on {fmt(nullB)} shuffled histories per target: shuffle which of the 32 elections count as wins, fit the first 21,
    take the top-ranked rule, score it on the last 11. In shuffled histories the top-ranked rule scored at least as well as the real one in
    {fmt(tg.T1.null.ge_real)} of {fmt(nullB)} (T1, {pct(tg.T1.null.ge_real / nullB, 1)}), {fmt(tg.T2.null.ge_real)} (T2, {pct(tg.T2.null.ge_real / nullB, 1)}) and
    {fmt(tg.T3.null.ge_real)} (T3, {pct(tg.T3.null.ge_real / nullB, 1)}).
    A shuffled history can look prophetic too: the top-ranked rule got all 11 right in {tg.T1.null.perfect_11_of_11} of {fmt(nullB)} shuffles for T1.
  </p>
  <CountHistogram series={histNull} bins={bins} title="Test scores of the top-ranked rule in shuffled histories"
    sub={`Share of ${fmt(nullB)} shuffled histories per target; the triangle marks the real top-ranked rule`}
    xLabel="elections right out of 11 (last 11 elections of the shuffled history)" yLabel="share of shuffled histories" realLegend="the real top-ranked rule"
    tipPrefix="Score " tipSuffix="" descId="fig-n3-null" />
  <details class="viz-table" open>
    <summary>Data table: test scores of the top-ranked rule in shuffled histories</summary>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Elections right (of {testN})</th>{T.map((t) => <th class="r">{t} shuffled histories</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {bins.map((b) => (
            <tr><td>{b.label}</td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r">{fmt(tg[t].null.top_correct_hist[b.key] ?? 0)} ({pct((tg[t].null.top_correct_hist[b.key] ?? 0) / nullB, 1)})</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
          <tr><td><strong>Real top-ranked rule</strong></td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r"><strong>{tg[t].top_rule.test_correct}/{testN}</strong></td>)}</tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Shuffles scoring at least as well</strong></td>{T.map((t) => <td class="r"><strong>{fmt(tg[t].null.ge_real)} ({pct(tg[t].null.ge_real / nullB, 1)}; {ci(tg[t].null.ge_real, nullB)})</strong></td>)}</tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <h2>Why fewer elections means shorter rules</h2>
  <p>
    Fitting 21 elections is easier than fitting 32. With fewer elections to get right, shorter rules suffice, and the original pool alone is often enough.
    The numbers below compare the same search on 21 elections and on all 32.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="plain">
      <thead>
        <tr><th rowspan="2">Target</th><th colspan="3">Fit 1900&ndash;1980 (21 elections), real history</th><th colspan="3">Fit all 32 elections, real history</th><th colspan="2">Shuffled histories: original pool alone (tier 1a) succeeds</th></tr>
        <tr><th>Result</th><th>Shortest rule</th><th>Perfect rules</th><th>Result</th><th>Shortest rule</th><th>Perfect rules</th><th>on 21 elections</th><th>on 32 elections</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {T.map((t) => {
          const r = tg[t];
          return (
            <tr>
              <td>{t}: {NAME[t]}</td>
              <td>{tier(r.real_label_21yr)}</td><td class="num">{r.min_k_21yr} facts</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.n_rules_21yr)}</td>
              <td>{tier(r.label_32yr)}</td><td class="num">{r.min_k_32yr ? `${r.min_k_32yr} facts` : '—'}</td><td class="num">{r.n_rules_32yr ? fmt(r.n_rules_32yr) : 'none'}</td>
              <td class="num">{pct(r.null.labels.conjA / r.null.B, 1)}</td><td class="num">{pct(nulls.n1[t].conjA.rate, 2)}</td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="sub">
    The top-ranked rules above have {tg.T1.top_rule.k} facts each even though shorter rules exist (T1 has one with {tg.T1.min_k_21yr}), because the frozen ranking prefers
    familiar facts first and length only later.
  </p>

  <h2>What this page did not do</h2>
  <div class="panel warn">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The shuffled-history comparison used {fmt(nullB)} shuffles per target,</strong> not the 20,000 originally planned. Finding the top-ranked rule exactly is slow when thousands of rules fit
        the 21 training elections, and the run took more than three hours. With {fmt(nullB)} shuffles, a share near 50% is known to within about &plusmn;2 percentage points.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The second split from the project plan was not run:</strong> fitting 1900&ndash;1990 and scoring 1992&ndash;2024. It was not part of the pre-declared runs.
      </li>
      <li>The test uses a single split, 1900&ndash;1980 versus 1984&ndash;2024, chosen in advance.</li>
      <li>Rules containing a club that no longer exists are flagged where they appear (the T1 top-ranked rule has one) and are not removed.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
